(a) If the Finance and Administrative Services Department deems it necessary to conduct an audit of the records and books of the licensee, the Department shall notify the licensee of the date, time and place of the audit. The Finance and Administrative Services Department may designate the person to perform any audit authorized in this Code. The licensee shall cooperate with such audit or forfeit any license issued under this division.
(b) All licensed establishments must maintain the following records for a three-year period and make them available for audit within five business days at the licensed premises upon request:
(1) Monthly income and operating statements; quarterly income and operating statements;
(2) Daily sales receipts showing alcoholic beverage and food sales broken down separately (this requirement does not apply to package beer and wine licensees) and daily cash register receipts such as Z-tapes or guest tickets;
(3) All invoices for alcoholic beverage and food purchases for each month, quarter, and year;
(4) Monthly state sales and use tax reports; and
(5) Federal and state income tax returns with all form 1099s.
(c) The Finance and Administrative Services Department may waive all or some of the requirements of subsection (b) of this section, if the Department finds that no such records exist; and it is not financially practical based on the net income of the licensed establishment to require them to keep such records.
(Code 1979, § 9-4-14.1; Code 1998, § 6-101; Ord. No. 17-2000, 12-4-2000; Ord. No. 07-2003, § 6-101, 7-7-2003; Ord. No. 05-2023, § I, 7-10-2023)

